ificial suffering insufficiently explained.","critique":"Strongest feature: The reply properly demands a resurrection-specific inferential bridge, observing that even a justified claim about God choosing ideal action or human incarnation would not automatically determine the probability of resurrection as the chosen method of authentication. Principal limitation: Its categorical dismissal overlooks Christopoulos’s explicit proposal that resurrection fittingly validates incarnation, while Dillahunty’s competing expectations about perfect divine disclosure, obscurity, and sacrificial means remain asserted more than independently defended. Live burden: Dillahunty must compare the proposed authentication rationale with plausible alternative divine actions and explain why a perfectly acting God would be unlikely to select resurrection under the stipulated purposes, rather than merely noting that incarnation alone is insufficient. Locked score: The score of 76, within the strong or competent band, credits relevant contact and a sound demand for an additional conditional bridge while reflecting incomplete engagement, underdeveloped divine expectations, and only moderate logical execution.","tags":[]},"con-alternatives-burden-preserved":{"role":"Major direct reply","words":"Defeating one natural explanation does not establish resurrection; the affirmative must still warrant its historical data, priors, and comparative likelihoods.","critique":"Strongest feature: The reply correctly preserves the affirmative burden and explains that failure to establish a selected naturalistic alternative does not automatically validate resurrection, especially when the reliability of the underlying biblical accounts remains independently disputed. Principal limitation: The courtroom analogy can obscure that a genuinely exhaustive probabilistic comparison may rationally favor one hypothesis without deductively disproving every competitor, so the point succeeds only against elimination-based 