{"schemaVersion":"1.0-assessment-production-post-canary-batch-13-publication-timeout-recovery-output","protocolId":"assessment-production-post-canary-batch-13-publication-timeout-field-disjoint-recovery","shardId":"debate-87-shard-2","debateNumber":"87","debateId":"christopoulos-dillahunty-resurrection-historical-event-2024","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","completedAt":"2026-08-26T00:00:00-04:00","summary":"Christopoulos presented a cumulative probabilistic resurrection case, while Dillahunty’s source challenges and distinctions between sincerity, likelihood, and warranted belief preserved rational uncertainty.","representativeQuotes":{"pro":{"sourceMoveId":"pro-comparative-likelihood-ratio","text":"a mixture of evidence for the miracle and against the competing theories is important","context":"Christopoulos used this formulation to explain why resurrection must be evaluated comparatively against competing explanations."},"con":{"sourceMoveId":"con-costly-commitment-not-truth","text":"you can be sincere and be sincerely wrong","context":"Dillahunty used this distinction to argue that costly commitment may establish sincerity without establishing the resurrection belief’s truth."}}}
