rately uncertain historical claims."}},{"id":"ai-con-premise-3","text":"Narrative agreement can arise from literary dependence or shared tradition, while disagreement can reflect memory or redaction; neither feature establishes independence without a source-specific transmission model.","novelty":{"classification":"repairs","sourceMoveIds":["con-source-account-disparities","con-source-corroboration-deficit"],"explanation":"This makes the source critique symmetrical by refusing to treat either agreement or disparity as automatically decisive."}},{"id":"ai-con-premise-4","text":"Sincerity, conversion, and costly persistence discriminate against conscious invention by some claimants but remain compatible with mistake, visionary experience, identity error, memory development, and transmission distortion.","novelty":{"classification":"extends","sourceMoveIds":["con-sincerity-transmission-limits","con-costly-commitment-not-truth"],"explanation":"This concedes the limited evidential value of costly commitment while explaining why it does not establish resurrection."}},{"id":"ai-con-premise-5","text":"The skeptic need not establish one complete natural history; the affirmative bears the comparative burden, and wide uncertainty over several natural processes can rationally prevent resurrection from becoming more probable.","novelty":{"classification":"extends","sourceMoveIds":["con-alternatives-burden-preserved","con-rational-uncertainty-option"],"explanation":"This clarifies why uncertainty distributed among multiple rivals can defeat the motion without proving any single rival."}}],"conclusion":{"id":"ai-con-conclusion","text":"Until the evidential chain and comparative likelihoods are supported with enough independence and precision to overcome reasonable uncertainty, withholding judgment is better warranted than treating resurrection as the most probable explanation.","novelty":{"classification":"extends","sourceMoveIds":["con-source-corroboration-deficit","con-alternatives-burden-preserved","con-rational-uncertainty-option"],"explanation":"This draws a restrained conclusion of non-establishment rather than claiming that resurrection was disproved."}},"newArguments":[{"id":"ai-con-new-1","title":"Dependence Penalty for Evidence Bundles","text":"The affirmative evidence should not be multiplied as though burial reports, empty-tomb narratives, appearances, and later witness traditions were independent observations. Shared communities, texts, theological expectations, and repeated retelling can generate several reported features from fewer underlying information channels. A formal dependence penalty would lower the cumulative force whenever claims travel together. The skeptical point is not that dependence proves falsehood, but that an apparently large evidence bundle may contain far less independent information than its item count suggests.","novelty":{"classification":"introduces","sourceMoveIds":[],"explanation":"This introduces an explicit dependence penalty for cumulative evidence, a method absent from the locked skeptical moves."}},{"id":"ai-con-new-2","title":"Imprecise Probability and Suspension","text":"Where numerical priors and likelihoods cannot be responsibly fixed, represent them as ranges rather than invented point values. If plausible ranges for source reliability, death confirmation, tomb occupancy, visionary experiences, and transmission overlap enough that different reasonable settings yield different leading hypotheses, no stable ranking follows. Suspension is then supported by model instability, not merely by missing information or personal incredulity. This also supplies a principled stopping rule: belief becomes warranted only when the same conclusion persists across the defensible parameter region.","novelty":{"classification":"introduces","sourceMoveIds":[],"explanation":"This adds an imprecise-probability framework and robustness criterion not articulated in the transcript inventory."}}]}}}
